component jogtarget """Where an axis WILL end up once its jog queue drains.

WHY THIS EXISTS. Operator 2026-08-18: "what i really want is a 3rd col in both
DROs that show the final destination assuming feed and accel was infinity if
the queue is cleared" ... "not distance to go, i find that useless".

Motion does not publish that number. The queued target is
axis->teleop_tp.pos_cmd, a variable inside the trajectory planner; every pin it
does export -- axis.N.pos-cmd, axis.N.teleop-pos-cmd, joint.N.pos-cmd -- is
teleop_tp.curr_pos, which is where the axis has ALREADY got to. The difference
between the two IS the queue, and it is exactly what the operator cannot see:
the wheel accepts counts far faster than the axis executes them, so an axis can
be tens of millimetres short of where it is going with nothing on screen
saying so.

HOW IT STAYS EXACT. It runs the planner's own arithmetic, axis.c:406,
    pos = teleop_tp.pos_cmd + delta * jog_scale
against the same inputs motion reads -- axis.N.jog-counts, axis.N.jog-scale,
axis.N.jog-enable -- in the servo thread, so no count is ever missed. A GUI
poll cannot do this: at 10 Hz it would drop counts and drift without bound.

HOW IT RE-SYNCS. Whenever the axis is not being fed AND has stopped moving,
there is by definition nothing queued, so target is snapped back to pos-cmd.
That costs nothing when the two already agree and it is what recovers the
number after a JOG_STOP, which abandons the planner's target without telling
anyone (axis_jog_abort, axis.c:321-333).

The value is MACHINE coordinates, same frame as axis.N.pos-cmd. The DRO
subtracts g5x_offset and g92_offset to show it in the active WCS.""";

pin in s32 counts "axis.N.jog-counts -- the same counts motion is fed";
pin in float scale "axis.N.jog-scale -- mm (or deg) per count";
pin in bit enable "axis.N.jog-enable -- counts only accumulate while this is true";
pin in float pos_cmd "axis.N.pos-cmd -- where the axis has actually got to";

pin out float target "where it ends up once the queue drains, MACHINE coords";
pin out float queued "target - pos_cmd, the outstanding distance";

variable int last_c = 0;
variable int started = 0;
variable double prev_pos = 0.0;
variable int still_n = 0;

function _ "Mirror the planner's jog target";
license "GPL";
;;

#include <rtapi_math.h>

FUNCTION(_) {
    int d;

    if (!started) {
        last_c = counts;
        target = pos_cmd;
        prev_pos = pos_cmd;
        queued = 0.0;
        started = 1;
        return;
    }

    d = counts - last_c;
    last_c = counts;

    /* Same line as axis.c:406. The enable check is axis.c:394 -- counts only
       land while the axis is selected. */
    if (enable && d != 0) {
        target += d * scale;
    }

    /* NOTHING ARRIVING AND NOTHING MOVING == NOTHING QUEUED.
       NOT gated on `enable`: the selected axis sits enabled and idle most of
       the time, and gating on it left target stuck at whatever it happened to
       hold on the first pass -- seeded 0 against a pos-cmd of -54.57 because
       HAL pins start at zero and this runs before motion has written one.
       A DWELL, not a single cycle: the count arrives one servo cycle before
       the axis starts moving, so an instant snap would erase the target that
       count had just created. still_n counts consecutive cycles with no
       counts AND no movement; 100 of them is 0.1 s at the 1 kHz servo rate,
       far longer than that gap and far shorter than any real queue.
       This is also what recovers the number after a JOG_STOP, a mode change,
       an e-stop or a g-code move -- none of which come through jog-counts. */
    if (d == 0 && fabs(pos_cmd - prev_pos) < 1e-9) {
        if (still_n < 100) still_n++;
    } else {
        still_n = 0;
    }
    if (still_n >= 100) {
        target = pos_cmd;
    }
    prev_pos = pos_cmd;

    queued = target - pos_cmd;
}
